### 2018A&A...617A.120M: Tracing the cold and warm physico-chemical structure of deeply embedded protostars: IRAS 16293-2422 vs. VLA 1623-2417 (2018; star_formation, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What factors determine the cold and warm chemical structure of two Class 0 protostars, IRAS 16293-2422 and VLA 1623-2417, as seen in key molecular tracers?
- step 2: Model DCO+ peak position with chemical network
  - method: Steady-state analytic H2D+/DCO+ network with CO freeze-out drop abundance, passed through RATRAN to synthetic cubes, temperature/density profiles scaled by arbitrary factors [4.1.1: "a simple steady-state, analytic chemical network that accounts for the basic reactions leading to the production and destruction of is used."]
  - software: RATRAN
  - parameter: T_sub = 20–40 K, n_de = 1e5–1e8 cm-3, X[CO] = 1e-7–1e-4 [4.1.1: "we explore the parameter ranges of $T_{\rm sub}$ = 20–40 K, $n_{\rm de}$ = 10$^{5}$–10$^{8}$ cm$^{-3}$ and $X[\ce{CO}]$ = 10$^{-7}$ – 10$^{-4}$."]
  - assumption: cosmic-ray ionization rate 1.26e-17 s-1; ortho-to-para H2 lower limit 1e-3; sources centred on and dominated by A [4.1.1: "The ortho-to-para ratio is set to have a lower limit of 10$^{-3}$ at low temperatures"]
  - fitting: By-eye fit of radial profile [4.1.1: "A by eye fit of the chemical model to the observations is used to constrain the decrease in the temperature profile"]
  - validation: Consistency with DCO+ results of VLA 1623 in Murillo et al. 2015 and with line-ratio temperatures [4.1.2: "This temperature is consistent with the chemical modelling of the peak position."]
- step 3: Derive T and n from line ratios (DCO+, c-C3H2)
  - method: RADEX non-LTE calculations compared to observed ratios, with peak intensities converted from Jy/beam to K [4.1.2: "Using RADEX (cite: vandertak2007), we performed non-LTE excitation and radiative transfer calculations to constrain the temperature and density"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: IRAS 16293: N=3e12 cm-2, width 1.0 km/s; VLA 1623: N=2e12 cm-2, width 0.7 km/s [4.1.2: "We adopt a line width of 1.0 km s$^{-1}$ and a column density of 3 $\times$ 10$^{12}$ cm$^{-2}$"]
  - assumption: ortho/para ratio of 3 for c-C3H2; molecular data from LAMDA [4.2: "To convert to the total (ortho + para) column density, an o/p ratio of 3 was used."]
  - validation: APEX single-dish ratio used as cross-check for VLA 1623 [4.1.2: "The APEX observations are used to double check if this is the kinetic temperature of the bulk of the emission at the disk-envelope interface."]
- step 4: Test non-detection of N-bearing lines in VLA 1623
  - method: Predict ALMA peak intensities from single-dish detections via RADEX for extended and compact (1") emission [4.4: "Two cases are explored, extended and compact emission concentrated in a 1$\ensuremath{''}$ region."]
  - software: RADEX
- result: Temperature reduction factor, VLA 1623 = 1.5 ± 0.2 dimensionless [4.1.1: "The factor is constrained to be 1.5+/-0.2 for VLA 1623 and 3.0+/-0.2 for IRAS 16293."]
- result: Temperature reduction factor, IRAS 16293 = 3.0 ± 0.2 dimensionless [4.1.1: "The factor is constrained to be 1.5+/-0.2 for VLA 1623 and 3.0+/-0.2 for IRAS 16293."]
- result: DCO+ 5-4/3-2 ratio, IRAS 16293 = 0.6 ± 0.1 dimensionless [4.1.2: "a ratio 5–4/3–2 = 0.6 $\pm$ 0.1 is obtained from the PILS Band 7 observations and the SMA 230 GHz observations"]
- result: Kinetic temperature from DCO+, IRAS 16293 = 20–23 ± not stated K [4.1.2: "a kinetic temperature between 20 and 23 K can be inferred for IRAS 16293"]
- result: DCO+ 5-4/3-2 ratio, VLA 1623 ALMA = 1.0 ± 0.1 dimensionless [4.1.2: "the ALMA 12m array observations provide 5–4/3–2 = 1.0 $\pm$ 0.1"]
- result: Kinetic temperature from DCO+, VLA 1623 ALMA = 30–55 ± not stated K [4.1.2: "This line brightness temperature ratio implies a kinetic temperature between 30 and 55 K"]
- result: DCO+ ratio, VLA 1623 APEX = 0.2 ± 0.03 dimensionless [4.1.2: "The APEX data give a much lower line ratio, 5–4/3–2 = 0.2 $\pm$ 0.03."]
- result: Kinetic temperature, VLA 1623 APEX = 12–19 ± not stated K [4.1.2: "well reproduced by a kinetic temperature of 12 to 19 K"]
- result: Kinetic temperature at c-C3H2 south peak, IRAS 16293 = 120–155 ± not stated K [4.2: "We find that the temperature for the south peak, corresponding to the outflow cavity of IRAS 16293 A , is between 120–155 K."]
- limitation (authors): Simple chemical model cannot explain inner DCO+ 5-4 emission; single physical profile adopted [4.1.1: "our simple chemical model cannot fully explain the inner part of the 5–4 emission"]
- not stated (card): CASA version and calibration/imaging details for VLA 1623 Cycle 0 and Cycle 2 data; Weighting, robust, cell and image size, and CLEAN parameters; Continuum subtraction method and line-free channels; Moment-map integration velocity ranges and masks; Exact RADEX grid ranges and how uncertainties on temperature were derived; Details of by-eye model fitting, including the chemical network settings (i…

### 2017ApJ...835..174S: Spatially resolved CO SLED of the Luminous Merger Remnant NGC 1614 with ALMA (2017; nearby_galaxies, milky_way_ism; observational_alma_primary)
- question: How do the distribution, kinematics and excitation of multiple CO transitions (J=1-0 to 6-5) differ between the starburst ring and outer disk of NGC 1614, and what heats the gas?
- step 3: Single-phase RADEX chi2 grid
  - method: Grid search of Tkin, n(H2), N(H2) minimizing chi2 against radial line fluxes [4.1 Single-phase RADEX Model: "varied the parameters until the residuals between the observed line fluxes and the modeled line fluxes are minimized in a $\chi^2$ sense"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: Tkin 10-300 K (1 K), n 10^2-10^7 cm^-3, N(H2) 10^20.5-10^23.5 cm^-2 [4.1 Single-phase RADEX Model: "We varied the gas kinetic temperature within a range of $T_{\rm{kin}}$ = 10 - 300 K (d$T_{\rm{kin}}$ = 1 K)"]
  - assumption: expanding sphere dv=200 km/s, Tbg=2.73 K, [CO]/[H2]=3e-4 [4.1 Single-phase RADEX Model: "an expanding sphere geometry ($dv$ = 200 km s$^{-1}$), the cosmic microwave background temperature ($T_{\rm{bg}}$ = $T_{\rm CMB}$ = 2.73 K)"]
  - fitting: chi2 minimization on a grid; 90% confidence ranges [5.4.1 Two-phase ISM model: "The 90% confidence ranges of $T_{\rm{warm}}$ are 300 - 370"]
  - validation: Reconstructed CO SLEDs compared with observations [4.1 Single-phase RADEX Model: "We reconstructed the best-fit CO SLED from models in order to compare with the observational data"]
- step 5: PDR modelling
  - method: PDR Toolbox grid for n and G0 using 90% range of 2n_H2 from RADEX [4.3 PDR Model: "We varied $n$ within the 90% confidence range of 2$n_{\rm{H_2}}$ derived by the single-phase RADEX"]
  - software: PDR Toolbox
  - parameter: G0 10^-0.5 - 10^6.5 (step 10^0.25) [4.3 PDR Model: "$G_{\rm{0}}$ within a range of 10$^{-0.50}$ - 10$^{6.50}$"]
  - assumption: n_H negligible relative to 2n_H2 [4.3 PDR Model: "Here we assumed that the $n_{\rm{H}}$ is negligible compared to the 2$n_{\rm{H_2}}$ term"]
  - fitting: chi2 grid [4.1 Single-phase RADEX Model: "varied the parameters until the residuals between the observed line fluxes and the modeled line fluxes are minimized in a $\chi^2$ sense"]
  - validation: Compared with G0 from IR luminosity prescription of Papadopoulos14 [4.3 PDR Model: "The derived $G_{\rm{0}}(L_{\rm{IR}})$ is $\lesssim$ 10$^{2.5}$, which is consistent with the average $G_{\rm{0}}$ derived from the CO SLED."]
- step 6: Two-phase RADEX model for ring
  - method: Fix cold component (~19 K from outer region), fit warm fraction r, Twarm, N(H2)warm for grids at three warm densities [5.4.1: "we minimize the number of free parameters by fixing the cold gas component using the observed line ratios at the outer region"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: Twarm 10-800 K; N 10^19-10^24; n_warm 10^2, 10^3, 10^4 [5.4.1: "$n_{\rm{warm}}$ = 10$^{2.0}$ (grid A), 10$^{3.0}$ (grid B), and 10$^{4.0}$ cm$^{-3}$ (grid C)"]
  - fitting: chi2 grid [4.3 PDR Model: "We varied $n$ within the 90% confidence range of 2$n_{\rm{H_2}}$ derived by the single-phase RADEX (d$n$ = 10$^{0.25}$ cm$^{-3}$)"]
  - validation: Min chi2 lower than single-phase [5.4.1: "which are smaller than the $\chi^2$ values of the single-phase modeling for the starburst ring"]
- step 7: Conversion factor, energetics and inflow balance
  - method: alpha_CO from RADEX N(H2) and CO luminosity; compare mechanical luminosities; estimate inflow rate from PVD flux [5.4.3: "we estimate molecular gas inflow rate (IFR) using"]
  - parameter: inclination 33°, alpha_CO=0.2 alpha_MW [5.5: "Here we adopt $\theta$ of 33$^\circ$ and $\alpha_{{\rm CO}}$ of 0.2$\alpha_{{\rm MW}}$"]
- result: Ring single-phase Tkin / log G0 = ~42 K; G0~10^2.7 ± not stated K [abstract: "$T_{\rm{kin}}$ $\sim$ 42 K, and $G_{\rm{0}}$ $\sim$ 10$^{2.7}$"]
- result: Outer disk Tkin / G0 = ~22 K; G0~10^0.9 ± not stated K [abstract: "$T_{\rm{kin}}$ $\sim$ 22 K, and $G_{\rm{0}}$ $\sim$ 10$^{0.9}$"]
- result: alpha_CO(1-0) at ring = 0.2-0.5 (×3e-4/[CO]/[H2]) ± not stated Msun (K km/s pc^2)^-1 [5.3: "The derived $\alpha_{\rm{CO(1-0)}}$ at the starburst ring is 0.2 - 0.5"]
- result: Inflow rate = 54 ± factor of three Msun/yr [5.5: "The derived IFR is 54 $M_{\odot}$ yr$^{-1}$ with the uncertainty by a factor of three"]
- result: Depletion time = 17.7 to 56.6 ± 4.6 and 9.3 Myr [5.5: "ranges from 17.7 $\pm$ 4.6 Myr ($\alpha_{\rm{CO}}$ = $\alpha_{\rm{MW}}$) to 56.6 $\pm$ 9.3 Myr"]
- limitation (authors): Missing flux on scales >4.6'' (1.5 kpc) [2.3.1: "we ignore the missing flux effect on structures smaller than 4.6$''$"]
