### 2017A&A...605A..21C: Increased H<SUB>2</SUB>CO production in the outer disk around HD 163296 (2017; protoplanetary_disks, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What is the distribution of H2CO in the HD 163296 disk, and what does it say about gas-phase versus solid-phase (grain-surface) formation routes?
- step 5: Model line emission with parameterized abundances
  - method: LIME non-LTE radiative transfer on Mathews2013 disk model; four abundance parameterizations (low-temperature, power-law, temperature step, radial step); synthetic visibilities via vis_sample; chi2 minimization on normalized visibilities [3.2 Modeling H2CO and C18O emission: "Goodness of fit for each model was determined by $\chi^2$ minimization between the normalized visibilities of the model and the visibilities of the data."]
  - software: LIME, vis_sample
  - parameter: R_in = 50 AU for H2CO; Tc 12–36 K in 2 K steps; Rc 210–410 AU (H2CO), 70–350 AU (C18O) in 20 AU steps; X1/X2 0.001–10 (0.1–1000 for C18O) [3.2.3 Temperature step-abundance model: "The change-over temperature $T_{\rm c}$ was tested in the range 12 – 36 K in steps of 2 K."]
  - parameter: Reference abundances 1e-12 (H2CO), 1e-7 (C18O) [3.2 Modeling H2CO and C18O emission: "The H$_2$CO reference abundance was set to $X = 1.0 \ \times \ 10^{-12}$."]
  - assumption: i = 44 deg, PA = 133 deg, d = 122 pc; OPR = 3 at T>=200 K and exponentially lower below; H2 collision partner; dust continuum optically thin [3.2 Modeling H2CO and C18O emission: "The disk inclination, position angle, and distance are set to $i$ = 44.0$^{\circ}$, P.A. = 133.0$^{\circ}$, and $d$ = 122 pc."]
  - fitting: Grid search with chi2 minimization on visibilities; reduced chi2 [Table 2: "$\chi^2$ values are reduced by the number of points and free parameters in each model."]
  - validation: Compare model and data radial profiles, spectra, integrated intensity maps and residuals [3.2.5 H2CO and C18O abundance: "Integrated intensity maps of the best-fit models were compared to integrated intensity maps of observed H$_2$CO 3$_{03}$–2$_{02}$ and C$^{18}$O 2–1 data."]
- result: Best-fit H2CO radial step change-over radius Rc = 270 ± 20 AU [3.2.4 Radial step-abundance model: "Best-fit parameters are a change-over radius $R_{\rm c}$ = 270$\pm$20 AU and an abundance ratio $X_1/X_2 = 0.5$."]
- result: H2CO X1/X2 = 0.5 ± not stated dimensionless [3.2.4 Radial step-abundance model: "Best-fit parameters are a change-over radius $R_{\rm c}$ = 270$\pm$20 AU and an abundance ratio $X_1/X_2 = 0.5$."]
- result: C18O radial step Rc and depletion factor = 290 AU; factor 10 ± 20 AU AU [3.2.4 Radial step-abundance model: "Best-fit C$^{18}$O models have $R_{\rm c}$ = 290$\pm$20 AU and $X_1/X_2 =$10"]
- result: H2CO low-temperature model threshold T = 24 ± 2 K [3.2.1 Low-temperature abundance model: "The best fit for the normalized low-temperature model for H$_2$CO has a threshold temperature of 24$\pm$2 K"]
- result: H2CO temperature step Tc = 16 ± 2 K [3.2.3 Temperature step-abundance model: "The best-fit H$_2$CO model has a change-over temperature $T_{\rm c} = 16 \pm 2$ K"]
- result: C18O temperature step Tc = 32 ± 2 K [3.2.3 Temperature step-abundance model: "C$^{18}$O is best fit by a change-over temperature $T_{\rm c} = 32 \pm 2$ K"]
- limitation (authors): Inner 50 AU cannot be modeled due to low resolution and dust optical depth [3.2 Modeling H2CO and C18O emission: "The inner region ($<$50 AU) cannot be properly modeled here due to the low resolution of the observations"]
- limitation (authors): Matched-filter fluxes are only lower limits [3.1.1 Matched filter detections: "The response of the template is limited by how well it spatially matches the emission, thus making the derived line fluxes lower limits."]
- limitation (authors): Parameterized models include no chemistry; formation route cannot be distinguished [4 Discussion: "It is difficult to distinguish which formation route is responsible for this modest increase in abundance."]
- not stated (card): Line-free channel ranges used for continuum subtraction and self-calibration; How the matched-filter response was normalized and the exact uv taper; Exact LIME settings (number of grid points, photons, resolution); Gas-to-dust ratio and dust opacity details inherited from the Mathews2013 model; Whether CASA version was specified (not given); Exact chi2 definition and uv binning for the model-data…

### 2013Sci...341..630Q: Imaging of the CO Snow Line in a Solar Nebula Analog (2013; protoplanetary_disks, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Where is the CO snow line in the TW Hya disk, as traced by the inner edge of N2H+ J=4-3 emission?
- step 3: Fit N2H+ radial column density distribution
  - method: Grid of RATRAN synthetic visibilities with power-law column density between Rin and Rout; chi2 minimization on real and imaginary visibilities [N2H+ line modeling: "The best-fit model is obtained by minimizing $\chi^2$, the weighted difference between the data and the model with the real and imaginary part of the complex visibility"]
  - software: RATRAN
  - parameter: free: Rin, Rout, p, N100; fixed sigma_s=3.2, sigma_m=100 [N2H+ line modeling: "We fix the vertical surface boundary $\sigma_s$ to 3.2 and the midplane boundary $\sigma_m$ to 100"]
  - assumption: Keplerian rotation; geometry fixed from earlier work [main text: "We assumed the disk material orbits the central star in Keplerian motion"]
  - fitting: chi2 grid search, 3 sigma errors [Table S2: "Errors within 3$\sigma$."]
  - validation: repeated for each z_big; inner radius varies <5 AU; residual channel maps [N2H+ line modeling: "Across this range of models, the inner radius varies by less than 5 AU"]
- step 4: Convert inner edge to CO freeze-out temperature and check with 13CO/C18O
  - method: Read midplane temperature at Rin; fit SMA 13CO and C18O with CO between freeze-out boundary and photodissociation [CO distribution: "CO is assumed to be present in the disk between a lower boundary set by the CO freeze-out temperature derived from the N$_2$H$^+$ modeling"]
  - software: RATRAN, MIRIAD
  - assumption: standard isotope ratios, 17 K freeze-out [CO distribution: "assuming standard isotope ratios and CO freeze-out at the N$_2$H$^+$ inner edge temperature of 17 K"]
  - fitting: same procedure as N2H+ [CO distribution: "The CO abundance structure was optimized using the same procedure as for N$_2$H$^+$ above."]
  - validation: fiducial structure reproduces isotopologue fluxes [CO distribution: "the best-fit CO abundance distribution fits the CO isotopologue observations well when using the fiducial disk structure"]
- result: N2H+ inner radius (fiducial z_big=3H) = 30 ± +1/-2 (3 sigma) AU [Table S2: "| 3.0 | 30$^{+1}_{-2}$ | 16–17"]
- result: CO freeze-out temperature at inner edge = 17 ± not stated K [main text: "the best-fit N$_2$H$^+$ inner radius corresponds to a CO midplane snow line at a temperature of 17 K"]
- result: N2H+ column density at 100 AU (z_big=3H) = 1.6e14 ± 0.3e14 cm^-2 [Table S2: "(1.6$\pm$0.3) $\times$10$^{14}$"]
- result: N2H+ outer radius (z_big=3H) = 140 ± 10 AU [Table S2: "| 3.0 | 30$^{+1}_{-2}$ | 16–17 | $-$2.0$^{+0.5}_{-0.7}$ | (1.6$\pm$0.3) $\times$10$^{14}$ | 140$\pm$10 |"]
- limitation (authors): Vertical disk structure is uncertain; vertical N2H+ boundaries fixed [Physical model: "The details of the vertical structure in the TW Hya disk are therefore uncertain"]
- not stated (card): Imaging parameters (weighting, robust, cell, image size, masking, deconvolver); Line-free channel selection and self-cal solution intervals; Grid ranges and step sizes for Rin, Rout, p, N100; Full disk model density/temperature structure files; Flux scale uncertainty for the ALMA data

### 2018A&A...612L..11S: Detection of CI line emission towards the oxygen-rich AGB star omi Ceti (2018; evolved_stars, astrochemistry; multi_facility_alma_minor)
- question: Is CI emission present in the envelope of the O-rich AGB star omi Cet, and does it trace external ISRF dissociation or internal UV from the binary companion?
- step 2: Shell chemistry plus non-LTE RT model
  - method: Chemical model gives 12C and 13C abundance profiles used as input to Monte Carlo non-LTE RT [4.1.1 The circumstellar model: "We used the $^{12}$C and $^{13}$C abundance distributions that are shown in Fig. [3](#Chemi) as input files in the mcp."]
  - software: mcp, UCL_CHEM-type CSE chemical model (McElroy13)
  - parameter: mass-loss 2.5e-7 Msun/yr, v_exp 2.5 km/s, 12CO/H2=5e-4, 13CO/H2=5e-5 [Chemical modelling results: "We assumed the initial fractional abundances of $^{12}$CO/H$_2$=5$\times$10$^{-4}$ reported for omi Cet (RS01)"]
  - assumption: Spherical uniformly expanding envelope, ISRF only external UV [4.1.1 The circumstellar model: "we assume a uniformly expanding spherical envelope which is formed due to a constant mass-loss"]
  - validation: CO(2-1,3-2,4-3) JCMT lines reasonably reproduced [4.1.1 The circumstellar model: "Although the model reasonably reproduces the CO observations"]
- step 4: Derive CI column density near Mira B
  - method: RADEX with dilution correction; column density is the only free parameter [4.1.3 The CI abundance in the postulate…: "The only free parameter in this modelling is the CI column density."]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: T_kin 170 K, n(H2) 1.9e6 cm-3, line width 9.5 km/s, T_bg 2.73 K [Table 3: "| 2.73 | 170 | 1.9E6 | 9.5 | 1.1E19 |"]
  - assumption: Emission region 0.4 arcsec; dilution factor ~0.001; position r=60 AU [4.1.3 The CI abundance in the postulate…: "With a telescope beam size of $\sim$13$''$ and assuming an emission region of 0.4$''$, we get a dilution factor of $(0.4/13)^2\sim 0.001$."]
  - fitting: Match corrected T_mb of 54 K [4.1.3 The CI abundance in the postulate…: "This leads to a corrected main beam temperature of 0.054 / 0.001 = 54 K."]
  - validation: T_R changes <10% over n_H2 1e6-1e12 and T_kin 100-500 K [4.1.3 The CI abundance in the postulate…: "T$_{\rm R}$ does not change more than 10 $\%$ over ranges of $1\times10^6<n_{\rm H_2}<1\times10^{12}$ cm$^{-3}$ and $100 <T_{\rm kin}<500$ K."]
- result: CI column density near Mira B = 1.1e19 ± not stated cm-2 [4.1.3 The CI abundance in the postulate…: "a CI column density of $1.1\times10^{19}$ cm$^{-2}$ is needed for the component near to Mira B"]
- result: CI/H2 with 100x overdensity = ~1.1e-4 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1.3 The CI abundance in the postulate…: "then our measurement implies a fractional abundance CI/H$_2$ $\sim1.1\times 10^{-4}$ in this location."]
- result: CI/CO with 100x H2 enhancement = ~0.2 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1.4 The CI/CO ratio: "we find a ratio CI/CO $\sim$ 0.2"]
- result: Peak CI abundance in shell model = 2.4e-5 ± not stated dimensionless [abstract: "a peak atomic fractional abundance of 2.4 $\times$ 10$^{-5}$"]
- limitation (authors): Single-dish data cannot constrain the complex outflow; the CSE model is crude [4.1.1 The circumstellar model: "it is not possible to constrain the complex outflow around omi Cet based on the single-dish observations"]
